sumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=6c394d5e63870c0f51da2ffcc26ddf3c6c594d243a3282469fbc16661e9b3abf&node=scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=6c394d5e63870c0f51da2ffcc26ddf3c6c594d243a3282469fbc16661e9b3abf&node=scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip&relation=prerequisites","prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=6c394d5e63870c0f51da2ffcc26ddf3c6c594d243a3282469fbc16661e9b3abf&view=arguments&node=scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip&assumptions=true"},"neighborhood_href":"/api/v1/neighborhood?manifest=6c394d5e63870c0f51da2ffcc26ddf3c6c594d243a3282469fbc16661e9b3abf&ref=sha256%3Af22708f3180068944c06829213f78f6ecfb65ef34f6c8decded774b95a041d92%2Fnodes%2Fscalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip","record":{"assumption_ids":[],"boundary":"Elementary finite-model deduction. It distinguishes exact maximizers at fixed size from near-ground pairs and supplies no rate, algorithm, formal replay or independent acceptance.","citation_ids":["authored_r10_finite_maximizer_uniqueness"],"evidence_ids":["ordinary_r10_proof_route_review_20261026"],"id":"scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip","kind":"claim","label":"Finite Gaussian maximizers are unique up to reversal","locator":"Long reasoning record #exact-finite-maximizers","source_id":"authored_long_comparisons_20261024","statement":"For every fixed positive integer n, the pure zero-field SK Hamiltonian with independent standard Gaussian pair couplings has almost surely exactly one maximizing pair {sigma,-sigma}. Configurations not related by global reversal differ in at least one pair product, so their energy difference is a nondegenerate Gaussian; a finite union excludes all such ties. This does not exclude nearly maximizing configurations at other overlaps in the large-n limit.","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:f22708f3180068944c06829213f78f6ecfb65ef34f6c8decded774b95a041d92/nodes/scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo